[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design.

  Choosing a service also forces a packaging decision this repo has so far avoided: there is no build step at all. Both
  paths change that fact or work around it.

  - [1mCDN script tag[0m — add one [38;5;153m<script>[39m to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. No npm, no bundler, nothing new to learn. Costs: a
    runtime dependency on the vendor's CDN being up, a version pinned in HTML, no tree-shaking, and no source-map
    upload, so stack traces from minified code stay unreadable.
  - [1mnpm + bundler (esbuild or Vite)[0m — install the SDK, add a build step, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes a module. Buys source-map upload
    (the thing that makes production stack traces actually useful), version pinning in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, and room to grow.
    Costs: a build step and a dist output where today you can open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly.

  Hard to reverse cheaply: introducing the bundler, because it changes how the app is served and how everyone runs it
  locally. Cheap: swapping which vendor sits behind our wrapper.

  My recommendation is npm + esbuild with Sentry. You said you're debugging failures reported after the fact — that work
  is mostly reading stack traces, and without source-map upload those traces point at minified columns. esbuild over
  Vite because this is one script with no framework; the config is a few lines.
